Subject: [PATCH] imx219: allow 6 sensors to share one reset GPIO (Auvidea J106)

On the Auvidea J106 carrier all 6 IMX219 CSI cameras share a SINGLE physical
reset line (TEGRA_MAIN_GPIO(R,5) = global sysfs gpio 461), released at boot by a
gpio-hog. The stock tegracam imx219 driver requires a per-sensor reset-gpios and
calls gpio_request() on it EXCLUSIVELY (one consumer per pin), then frees it on
power_put. That makes it impossible for more than one sensor to reference the
shared line (sensors 2..6 fail probe with -EBUSY/-16), which is exactly why the
device tree previously needed per-sensor *dummy* reset pins.

This mirrors how the TX1 R24.2.1 BSP worked (single shared reset toggled once,
not exclusively owned per sensor). With this patch all 6 sensors point their
reset-gpios at the one real line (461); the driver:

  - treats -EBUSY from gpio_request() as success, so a sibling sensor (or the
    gpio-hog) that already owns the shared line does not fail probe; and
  - never frees the shared reset line, so unbinding one sensor cannot release
    reset for the others (or free a descriptor it never owned).

Target: L4T R32.7.6 (kernel 4.9.337), drivers/media/i2c/imx219.c.
Apply from the kernel source root (the dir that contains kernel/nvidia):
    patch -p1 < patches/0001-imx219-share-reset-gpio-j106.patch

--- a/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx219.c
+++ b/kernel/nvidia/drivers/media/i2c/imx219.c
@@ -433,8 +433,9 @@
 	pw->avdd = NULL;
 	pw->iovdd = NULL;
 
-	if (likely(pw->reset_gpio))
-		gpio_free(pw->reset_gpio);
+	/* J106: reset line is shared by all 6 sensors - never free it here,
+	 * otherwise unbinding one sensor would release reset for the others
+	 * (or free a descriptor this instance never owned). */
 
 	return 0;
 }
@@ -489,9 +490,14 @@
 		goto done;
 	}
 
-	/* Reset or ENABLE GPIO */
+	/* Reset or ENABLE GPIO.
+	 * Auvidea J106: all 6 IMX219 share ONE physical reset line, so the
+	 * gpio may already be owned by a sibling sensor or by a gpio-hog
+	 * (-EBUSY). Treat that as success and just drive the shared line. */
 	pw->reset_gpio = pdata->reset_gpio;
 	err = gpio_request(pw->reset_gpio, "cam_reset_gpio");
+	if (err == -EBUSY)
+		err = 0;
 	if (err < 0) {
 		dev_err(dev, "%s: unable to request reset_gpio (%d)\n",
 			__func__, err);
